NAME
       console_theme, fb_setmode - console colours and screen resolution

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/syscall.h>

       typedef struct {
           uint32_t palette[16];
           uint32_t fg;
           uint32_t bg;
       } console_theme_t;

       syscall1(SYS_CONSOLE_THEME, &theme);
       syscall3(SYS_FB_SETMODE, op, a, b);

DESCRIPTION
       SYS_CONSOLE_THEME replaces the sixteen palette entries, the
       default text colour and the background, all as 0xRRGGBB.

       Colours already on screen are mapped from the old palette to the
       new one entry by entry, so what was red stays red and the
       contents of every terminal survive the change. A grid allocated
       afterwards takes the new colours, so a terminal opened later
       matches. The debug monitor follows the theme as well, and the
       kernel applies /etc/console.conf itself once the initramfs is
       mounted, before init runs.

       SYS_FB_SETMODE lists or sets the screen resolution. Operation 0
       fills in a structure with the modes the adapter offers and the
       one in use; operation 1 switches to the width and height given.
       The backbuffer is reallocated and the terminals reflowed, the
       same as after a font change.

       Mode setting needs a Bochs display adapter, which is what QEMU,
       Bochs and VirtualBox present. Real graphics chips have no driver
       here and return ENODEV.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success. Listing returns the count, or ENODEV where no
       adapter supports it.

ERRORS
       EINVAL   the mode is not one the adapter accepts
       ENODEV   this display cannot change mode
       EFAULT   the structure is outside the address space

SEE ALSO
       theme(1), mode(1), setfont(2), framebuffer(2)
